might have been sent earlier? Wait: The rule says \"treats the channel on which that first marker arrived as empty in the snapshot\". Means that for that incoming channel, the recorded state is empty (no messages). Even if there were pending messages in transit? In CL algorithm, the channel is considered empty because the marker passes through and any messages already in transit are counted elsewhere? Typically the channel is considered empty because the marker overtakes any messages in flight, and those messages are accounted for in the sender's snapshot. So we set recorded state = [] for that channel.\n\nNow we need to track counters.\n\nLet's enumerate events with timestamps.\n\nWe'll maintain per-process counters.\n\nInitially all counters 0.\n\nEvent 1: P2 sends application message m1 to P1. This is a send event for P2, so P2 counter increments +1 => c2=1. Also eventually P1 will receive m1, increment c1.\n\nEvent 2: P3 sends application message m2 to P1. P3 counter +1 => c3=1. Later P1 receives m2 -> c1+1.\n\nEvent 3: P1 sends application message m3 to P3. P1 counter +1 => c1=1. Later P3 receives m3 -> c3+1.\n\nEvent 4: P3 sends application message m4 to P2. P3 counter +1 => c3=2. Later P2 receives m4 -> c2+1.\n\nEvent 5: P1 initiates snapshot, records its local state, and sends its markers. At this point, what is P1's counter? We have increments from event 3 (send m3) gave c1=1. No other sends/receives yet for P1. So c1=1 at snapshot start. Record local state = 1.\n\nThen P1 sends markers on outgoing channels: to P2 and to P3. These are marker messages, not affecting counters.\n\nImmediately begins recording each incoming channel (from P2 and P3). So from now on, any application messages arriving on P1 from P2 or P3 will be recorded for that channel until the marker from that sender arrives.\n\nNote: The marker from P1 to P2/3 will travel; we need to consider ordering relative to other events.\n\nEvent 6: P2 sends application message m5 to P3. This is a send 