is marker, it should stop recording that channel. So channel P2->P3 recorded state consists of messages recorded before this marker, i.e., m5 (recorded at event 11). So final recorded state for channel P2->P3 = [m5].\n\nEvent 15: P2 receives the marker from P3. This is a marker on channel P3->P2. For P2, this is a marker on an incoming channel (the one where it was recording). So channel P3->P2 recorded state consists of messages recorded before this marker, i.e., m4 (recorded at event 12). So final recorded state for channel P3->P2 = [m4].\n\nEvent 16: P1 receives the marker from P2. This is a marker on channel P2->P1. For P1, this is a marker on an incoming channel (the one where it was recording). So channel P2->P1 recorded state consists of messages recorded before this marker, i.e., m1 (recorded at event 10). So final recorded state for channel P2->P1 = [m1].\n\nEvent 17: P1 receives the marker from P3. This is a marker on channel P3->P1. For P1, this is a marker on an incoming channel (the one where it was recording). So channel P3->P1 recorded state consists of messages recorded before this marker, i.e., m2 (recorded at event 13). So final recorded state for channel P3->P1 = [m2].\n\nNow we also need to consider outgoing channels for each process: The snapshot records local state and incoming channel states. Outgoing channel states are not directly recorded; they are inferred from senders' snapshots. But the problem asks \"recorded state of every directed channel\". Usually in CL algorithm, you record the state of each channel as the set of messages in transit at the instant of the cut, which can be derived from either side: for each directed channel, the recorded state is the set of messages that have been sent but not yet received at the time of the cut. In our simulation, we have recorded incoming channel states for each process. That's sufficient; we can present them as per direction.\n\nThus we have:\n\nLocal counters at snapshot:\n- P1: recorded at step 5 = 1 (initial snapshot). However after snapshot, P1 continues to receive messages and its counter increases, but the snapshot captures the value at the moment of initiation (before any later events). So local state for P1 = 1.\n\n- P2: recorded at step 8 = 2.\n\n- P3: recorded at step 9 = 3.\n\nNow channel recorded states (incoming to each process):\n\nFrom P2 to P1: [m1]\nFrom P3 to P1: [m2]\n\nFrom P1 to P2: empty (since P2 treated its first marker channel as empty). Indeed P2's first marker came from P1, so channel P1->P2 recorded state = [].\n\nFrom P1 to P3: empty (since P3's first marker came from P1). So channel P1->P3 = [].\n\nFrom P2 to P3: [m5] (recorded by P3)\n\nFrom P3 to P2: [m4] (recorded by P2)\n\nCheck consistency: A consistent cut means that for each directed channel, the number of messages sent minus received equals the size of recorded state (i.e., the cut includes all messages in transit). Equivalent condition: For each process, the sum of outgoing recorded messages plus its local state after sending etc matches incoming recorded messages plus its local state after receiving etc. More concretely, a consistent cut satisfies that for each process, the total number of messages sent up to the cut equals total number of messages received up to the cut plus the number of messages still in transit leaving the process (i.e., outgoing recorded). Another way: For each directed channel, the recorded state should be exactly the set of messages that have been sent but not yet received at the cut. Our recorded states reflect that.\n\nBut we need to verify that there is no missing message: Did any message get lost? Let's list all application messages and see where they end up.\n\nMessages:\n\nm1: sent by P2 at event1, received by P1 at event10. So at cut, m1 is in transit from P2 to P1. Recorded in channel P2->P1 as [m1]. Good.\n\nm2: sent by P3 at event2, received by P1 at event13. So in transit P3->P1, recorded as [m2]. Good.\n\nm3: sent by P1 at event3, received by P3 at event7. So by cut, m3 has already been delivered; not in transit. Should not appear in any channel state. Indeed none of the recorded states include m3. Good.\n\nm4: sent by P3 at event4, received by P2 at event12. So in transit P3->P2, recorded as [m4]. Good.\n\nm5: sent by P2 at event6, received by P3 at event11. So in transit P2->P3, recorded as [m5]. Good.\n\nAll messages accounted.\n\nNow check counters consistency: For each process, the local counter at snapshot should equal number of application messages sent up to that point plus number received up to that point (excluding those still in transit). Because each send increments counter, each receive increments counter. At snapshot time, some messages may be in transit (sent but not yet received). Those should not be double-counted.\n\nCompute for each process using our recorded data.\n\nProcess P1:\n\nSent messages up to snapshot initiation (event5): only m3 (sent at event3). Received messages up to that point: none yet (m1,m2 not yet received). So total events affecting counter up to snapshot: sends=1, receives=0 => counter should be 1. Indeed recorded local state =1. Good.\n\nProcess P2:\n\nSent messages up to its first marker receipt (event8). Sent: m1 (event1), m5 (event6). That's two sends. Received messages up to that point: none yet (m4 not yet received). So counter should be 2. Recorded local state =2. Good.\n\nProcess P3:\n\nSent messages up to its first marker receipt (event9). Sent: m2 (event2), m4 (event4). That's two sends. Received messages up to that point: m3 (event7). So total events = 2 sends + 1 receive =3. Counter =3. Recorded local state =3. Good.\n\nThus snapshot is consistent.\n\nNow produce JSON output.\n\nStructure:\n\n{\n \"local\": {\"P1\":1,\"P2\":2,\"P3\":3},\n \"channels\": {\n   \"P1>P2\": [],\n   \"P1>P3\": [],\n   \"P2>P1\": [\"m1\"],\n   \"P2>P3\": [\"m5\"],\n   \"P3>P1\": [\"m2\"],\n   \"P3>P2\": [\"m4\"]\n },\n \"consistent\": true\n}\n\nMake sure ordering o